today I'm going to tell you about how  other people can make us healthier and  we're perhaps used to the idea that  other people can make us mentally  healthy they can improve our happiness  and make us more satisfied with life but  what you may be not so used to thinking  about is how other people can make us  physically healthier so how they can  help us stave off disease improve our  immune system and even help us live  longer and to do this i'm going to give  you an example so i'd like to introduce  you to my grandmother marion greenaway  Marion has some pretty significant  health problems she's legally blind she  has a hearing deficit and some pretty  serious heart problems but despite that  she's doing really well for her age she  just turned 91 earlier this year that  puts her 10 years over the average life  expectancy in Australia and she still  lives in independent living an  independent living retirement village  which means that she's still looking  after herself in a way similar to she  was 10 years ago or even 20 years ago  and I think that a big part of Marion's  health and vitality really comes from  her very rich social life so Marion  lives in a retirement village that very  much encourages social activities and  interactions among its members she's a  member of many local clubs and actually  founded several local clubs and every  afternoon at four she gets together with  a friend across the road and they have a  glass of wine and they read together and  i was told that her most recent reading  venture was the book 50 shades of grey  and she told me that it didn't teach her  anything that she didn't already know  which I personally didn't need to know  but in fact Marion social life is so  good that she once had to put off an  appointment with her eye specialist from  december to january because she had too  many christmas parties to attend and it  turns out that these sorts of social  interactions this rich social life is  one of the best things that you can do  for adding years to your life so this is  an image of someone's social network of  friends on facebook and the more  connections you have the richer your  social network the healthier people tend  to be and its effect of having more  social connections is actually  comparable to a number of medical  interventions that were typically used  to thinking about as making us healthier  so these are odds ratios for living  longer given a particular medical  intervention so if you're overweight or  obese and you reduce your BMI and you  increase your chances of living longer  by up to twenty percent you get a  similar boost for getting vaccinated and  also for reducing your amount of  drinking if you often drink alcohol to  excess the big one obviously is quitting  smoking if you give up smoking then you  increase your chances of living longer  by up to fifty percent and when we  compared with this the effect of having  more social connections in your life the  difference between having one person  that you can talk to about your problems  versus being socially isolated then we  increase our chances of living longer by  up to sixty percent so that's an effect  that's comparable to or even slightly  better than these traditional medical  interventions that we typically think of  as making us healthier and it's  statistics like this that have lead  political scientists Robert Putnam to  conclude that if you smoke and belong to  no groups then statistically it's a  toss-up whether you should stop smoking  or start joining and that's because  groups give us some really important  things that are very protective for our  health and well-being they give us a  sense of social connection with other  people in a sense of social identity  when we think about identity  often we think about the things that  make us unique or different from other  people but in fact a big part of our  identities is wrapped up in the things  that we have in common with other people  or the group memberships that we share  so a big part of my identity is the fact  that I'm a woman and an academic and an  Australian which might make it slightly  strange that I have a picture of the new  zealand all blacks football team behind  me but these kinds of group memberships  are very important and the degree to  which people identify with these groups  or adopt them into themselves as  important characteristics of who they  are and the degree to which they feel a  sense of social connection or oneness  with those groups has very profound  impacts on people's health so people who  identify with groups tend to be  healthier and i'm going to give you an  example of a few studies that have  demonstrated this point that joining  groups are identifying with groups can  make us healthier so in this first study  they were interested in the issue of  getting older adults to drink more water  because dehydration is a big problem  among many people in aged care  facilities and what these researchers  did was they had half of the  participants join a water club or a  group about water so they got together  they talked about water they drank water  together and for the other half of  participants they did the same thing  they thought about what are they drank  water but they did it alone and these  researchers were interested in the  number of doctors visits that these  participants had in the 12 weeks after  this intervention and it turned out that  people who joined the water group were  half as likely to go to the doctor in  that 12-week period as people who were  in the alone group or in the alone  condition and it's not just in terms of  physical health that groups can be  beneficial it's also in terms of our  cognitive health so in this next study  I'll tell you about the researchers  weren't interested in the effect of  joining groups versus not they were  interested in the effects of identifying  with healthier or less healthy groups so  in this study they did it in a really  clever way they had they recruited  people who were aged 60 to 70 and they  told half of the participants that they  were recruiting p  aged 50 to 60 so these participants felt  as though they're on the higher end of  the age spectrum in this study and they  were self-identifying as being older  individuals for the other half of  participants they told them they were  recruiting people aged 70 to 80 so they  were on the younger age of the end  spectrum age spectrum so these  participants actually self-identified as  being younger and then they gave  everyone a test that very accurately  diagnoses the onset of dementia and a  score of 82 or below on this test  indicates that you have symptoms of  dementia on average people who  identified as being old scored below  this critical cutoff point whereas  people who self-identified as being  young scored above it they were  protected against the effects of  presenting with dementia and it's even  more striking when you look at the  percentage of people in each condition  who presented with symptoms of dementia  so fourteen percent of people in the  young condition who felt young presented  with symptoms of dementia compared with  seventy percent of people who identified  as being older so simply the act of  identifying with a group that we  consider as having cognitive deficits  versus identifying with a group that we  think is relatively protected is enough  to change our scores on these very  accurate tests that diagnose dementia so  what these studies are telling us is  that group identification and joining  groups is very important and protective  for our health and well-being and the  question that we're asking now is why so  what is it about groups that gives  people this protective benefit and there  are a range of different processes that  go into this summer very biological or  physiological it has to do with the  boost that your immune system gets from  being with other people and having these  regular social connections other  processes are more social so it has to  do with when you join groups you have  access to social support and social  capital people that you can rely on to  help you achieve the things that you  want in life I'm a social psychologist  so I'm particularly interested in the  psychological benefits that group  memberships can  give us so how do groups actually change  the way that we think about ourselves  and the world and how does that feed  into our own mental and physical health  and it turns out the group's give us a  range of different psychological  benefits that can be protective for our  health and well-being they give us a  sense of meaning so we feel as though  the world is worthwhile and our purpose  here is worthwhile they can help our  self-definition so they give us more of  a sense of who we are and what we want  out of life and what I'm particularly  interested in is that groups can  actually give us a sense of perceived  control over our lives so they can make  us feel agenta canned capable of  achieving the things that we want out of  life they can turn us from being passive  passengers in the passenger seat of life  to active go Gators who go out and able  to achieve the things that they want and  it turns out that a sense of control is  incredibly important and protective for  your mental and physical health I think  this has been touched on a little bit  already today and it's something that  John will continue to touch on in his  keynote later on just to give you one  example of an experiment that has  demonstrated this some psychologists  went into an aged care home and for half  of the participants they really  emphasized a sense of high personal  control so they told these people if you  want anything changed in this facility  if if you're unhappy with something or  want something done differently then you  have that power you're able to go out  and do that it's up to you and for the  other half of participants they said  everything's under control generally if  there's anything you would like changed  or done differently than the nurses are  here to help you just tell them and  they'll take care of everything so in  both cases a positive message just one  emphasizes a high degree of personal  control and the other emphasizes a low  degree of personal control the  researchers went back one year later and  they found that participants who were  empowered with a sense of high personal  control were happier they were more  engaged but critically they were fifty  percent more likely to still be alive  so in this case having a sense of  control was actually the difference  between life and death and I'll point  out that this fifty percent is  comparable to the effect of giving up  smoking that we saw earlier just having  a sense of control over your life so the  model that we're working from now is the  idea that identifying with groups having  group memberships can give us a sense of  personal control over our lives and for  that reason is protective and beneficial  for our mental and physical health and  we've been looking at this in a range of  different groups mostly around people  who have experienced adverse life  circumstances so we've looked at it in  religious people who have experienced a  life trauma in Republicans who have  experienced the 2012 u.s. presidential  election loss we've looked at it in  students who are facing their most most  stressful final year of exams and we've  even looked at it in the most vulnerable  of populations recovering drug and  alcohol addicts and in all of these  groups we find the same thing which is  that identifying with an important group  in your life gives you a sense of  control over your life and that control  can protect your health and well-being  even in these very adverse life  circumstances so I'm going to leave you  with a final thought and that is that  the things that we typically think of as  making us healthy these kinds of typical  medical interventions that we think of  might have led us to overlook a resource  that we have under our noses which is  other people the social connections we  make in the social relationships that we  have and when my grandmother goes to her  doctor for her checkup it's likely that  she's asked questions about these kinds  of medical factors what's a diet like  has she been exercising lately has she  had any injuries or illnesses it's  unlikely that she's going to be asked  questions like has she talked to anyone  recently when was the last time she  spoke with her family and friends has  she joined any new groups lately  and these questions are actually  incredibly important for understanding  our health and well-being and it turns  out that the answers might hold the key  to living happier healthier and even  longer lives thanks  you
 

